# Credentials for render/publish.py, render/bot.py and render/retract.py.
# Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# Use an app password (https://bsky.app/settings/app-passwords), never your
# account password. This file is the template; .env itself is gitignored.

BSKY_IDENTIFIER=you.bsky.social
BSKY_APP_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx

# publish.py also accepts BSKY_DM_IDENTIFIER / BSKY_HANDLE for the identifier,
# and BSKY_PASSWORD / BSKY_DM_APP_PASSWORD for the password. Those aliases exist
# so an .env written for something else still works — which is convenient and is
# also how you end up uploading as the wrong account without noticing.
#
# So pass --expect-account on every run:
#
#     python3 render/publish.py --video out.mp4 --env .env --expect-account you.bsky.social
#
# It refuses when the file holds a different account, and warns when you omit
# it. Exit code 31 (IDENTITY_MISMATCH) is that refusal; never retry around it.
#
# The bot takes the same flag and it matters more there, because the bot posts
# unattended:
#
#     python3 render/bot.py --env .env --expect-account yourbot.bsky.social --once
#
# Give the bot its OWN account. It is the only thing here that publishes without
# a human in the loop, and an app password scoped to a separate account is the
# difference between a bug posting as a bot and a bug posting as you.
